UPDATE public.stories 
SET content = 'A distinguished professor of philosophy traveled to a remote mountain monastery to meet with a famous Zen master. The professor had read extensively about Zen and came prepared to discuss its principles with the master. He arrived full of his own knowledge, eager to demonstrate his understanding and perhaps even debate with this simple monk.

The Zen master welcomed him warmly and invited him to sit for tea. As they settled into the simple tea room, the master began to pour tea into the professor''s cup. The professor watched as the cup filled to the brim. But the master did not stop pouring. The tea overflowed onto the saucer. Still the master poured. The tea spilled onto the table and began to drip onto the floor.

Finally, the professor could not restrain himself. "Stop! Stop!" he cried. "The cup is full! Can you not see? No more will go in! It is overflowing!"

The Zen master stopped pouring and set down the teapot. He looked at the professor with gentle eyes and said: "Like this cup, you are full—full of your own opinions, your own interpretations, your own certainties about what Zen is and is not. How can I show you Zen unless you first empty your cup? You have come here to learn, but you arrive already knowing. How can something new enter when there is no room?"

The professor sat back, stunned. He realized he had traveled all this way not to learn, but to confirm what he already believed. He had no space inside him for anything genuinely new, no humility before the mystery. His cup was already full. The most basic requirement for learning—emptiness, openness, genuine not-knowing—was absent. Until he could empty his cup, no teaching could reach him.'
WHERE title = 'The Empty Cup';

UPDATE public.stories 
SET content = 'In a small Japanese village, there lived a Zen master named Hakuin who was deeply revered for his wisdom and virtue. One day, a young unmarried girl in the village became pregnant. When her parents discovered this and demanded to know who the father was, the terrified girl, not wanting to reveal her actual lover (a young man from the fish market), falsely claimed it was the respected Zen master Hakuin.

The parents were outraged and scandalized. They stormed to Hakuin''s humble dwelling and confronted him with the accusation. Hakuin listened calmly to their fury and indignation, and when they finished, he said only two words: "Is that so?"

When the baby was born, the parents brought the infant to Hakuin, saying, "You are the father. This child is your responsibility." Hakuin accepted the baby without protest or defense, saying only: "Is that so?" He took the child into his care and, despite his advanced age and poverty, raised the baby with great tenderness. He begged for milk from neighbors, sang lullabies to soothe the infant''s crying, and carried the child everywhere he went.

Meanwhile, his reputation was destroyed. Students abandoned him. The village that once honored him now scorned him. Yet Hakuin said nothing in his own defense. He simply cared for the child with love and continued his practice.

A year passed. The young mother, tormented by guilt and moved by Hakuin''s silent acceptance, finally confessed the truth to her parents. The actual father was the young fish merchant. The parents were horrified by what they had done. They rushed to Hakuin''s home and prostrated themselves, begging forgiveness for the terrible injustice. They poured out apologies, explained the whole situation, and reached for the baby.

Hakuin handed the child back to them and said only: "Is that so?"

Whether in praise or blame, honor or disgrace, he maintained the same peaceful mind.'
WHERE title = 'Is That So?';

UPDATE public.stories 
SET content = 'A Cherokee elder was teaching his young grandson about life and the inner struggles that all people face. The old man had lived through much—war, loss, triumph, and tragedy—and he wanted to pass on what he had learned to the next generation.

"My child," he said, "there is a battle that rages inside me, and it rages inside you, and inside every person who walks this earth. It is a terrible fight between two wolves."

The boy''s eyes grew wide. "What are these wolves, grandfather?"

"One wolf," the elder continued, "is full of anger, envy, jealousy, sorrow, regret, greed, arrogance, self-pity, guilt, resentment, lies, false pride, and ego. This wolf sees enemies everywhere, trusts no one, and is never satisfied. It devours peace and spits out bitterness."

The boy shuddered at this description. "And the other wolf?"

The old man''s face softened. "The other wolf is joy, peace, love, hope, serenity, humility, kindness, benevolence, empathy, generosity, truth, compassion, and faith. This wolf sees beauty in the world, finds reasons for gratitude, and spreads light wherever it walks."

The grandson sat thoughtfully for a moment, then looked up at his grandfather with concern in his eyes. "This sounds like a terrible battle, grandfather. These two wolves are so different, so opposed to each other. They must fight viciously. Tell me, which wolf wins?"

The old Cherokee smiled and placed his weathered hand on the boy''s shoulder. "The one I feed," he said simply. "The one I feed."

The teaching was clear: we become what we nurture with our attention, our thoughts, our choices, and our actions every single day.'
WHERE title = 'The Two Wolves';